      "generated_tokens": 350,
      "hit_max_new_tokens": false,
      "nonempty_text": true,
      "repetition_onset": null,
      "substantive_text": "To solve how many pages Julie should read tomorrow, we need to follow these steps:\n1. Calculate how many pages Julie has already read.\n2. Determine how many pages she read yesterday.\n3. Calculate how many pages she read today.\n4. Calculate how many pages she has left to read.\n5. Determine how many pages she should read tomorrow.\nStep 1: Calculate how many pages Julie has already read.\nJulie is reading a 120-page book. Yesterday, she read 12 pages. Today, she read twice as many pages as yesterday, 24 pages in total number of pages she has read so far is:\n12 (yesterday) + 12 (today) = 24 pages\nStep 2: Determine how many pages she read yesterday.\nYesterday, Julie read 12 pages.\nStep 3: Calculate how many pages she read today.\nYesterday, Julie read 12 pages. Today, she read twice as many pages as yesterday.\nSo, the number of pages she read today is:\n2 x 12 = 24 pages\nStep 4: Calculate how many pages she has left to read.\nJulie is reading a 120-page book. She has already read 24 pages.\nSo, the number of pages she has left to read is:\n120 ( 24 = 96 pages\nStep 5: Determine how many pages she should read tomorrow.\nJulie wants to read half of the remaining pages tomorrow.\nSo, the number of pages she should read tomorrow is:\n96 / 2 = 48 pages\nTherefore, Julie should read 48 pages tomorrow.",
      "teacher_answer": "48",
      "teacher_answer_match": true,
      "teacher_text": "To determine how many pages Julie should read tomorrow, we need to follow these steps:\n1. Calculate how many pages Julie has already read.\n2. Determine how many pages she read yesterday.\n3. Calculate how many pages she read today.\n4. Calculate how many pages she has left to read.\n5. Determine how many pages she should read tomorrow.\nStep 1: Calculate how many pages Julie has already read.\nJulie is reading a 120-page book. Yesterday, she read 12 pages. Today, she read twice as many pages as yesterday.\nSo, the total number of pages she has read so far is:\n12 (yesterday) + 12 (today) = 24 pages\nStep 2: Determine how many pages she read yesterday.\nYesterday, Julie read 12 pages.\nStep 3: Calculate how many pages she read today.\nYesterday, Julie read 12 pages. Today, she read twice as many pages as yesterday.\nSo, the number of pages she read today is:\n2 x 12 = 24 pages\nStep 4: Calculate how many pages she has left to read.\nJulie is reading a 120-page book. She has already read 24 pages.\nSo, the number of pages she has left to read is:\n120 - 24 = 96 pages\nStep 5: Determine how many pages she should read tomorrow.\nJulie wants to read half of the remaining pages tomorrow.\nSo, the number of pages she should read tomorrow is:\n96 / 2 = 48 pages\nTherefore, Julie should read 48 pages tomorrow.<|end_turn|>",
      "teacher_valid_answer_tokens": 350,
      "text": "To solve how many pages Julie should read tomorrow, we need to follow these steps:\n1. Calculate how many pages Julie has already read.\n2. Determine how many pages she read yesterday.\n3. Calculate how many pages she read today.\n4. Calculate how many pages she has left to read.\n5. Determine how many pages she should read tomorrow.\nStep 1: Calculate how many pages Julie has already read.\nJulie is reading a 120-page book. Yesterday, she read 12 pages. Today, she read twice as many pages as yesterday, 24 pages in total number of pages she has read so far is:\n12 (yesterday) + 12 (today) = 24 pages\nStep 2: Determine how many pages she read yesterday.\nYesterday, Julie read 12 pages.\nStep 3: Calculate how many pages she read today.\nYesterday, Julie read 12 pages. Today, she read twice as many pages as yesterday.\nSo, the number of pages she read today is:\n2 x 12 = 24 pages\nStep 4: Calculate how many pages she has left to read.\nJulie is reading a 120-page book. She has already read 24 pages.\nSo, the number of pages she has left to read is:\n120 ( 24 = 96 pages\nStep 5: Determine how many pages she should read tomorrow.\nJulie wants to read half of the remaining pages tomorrow.\nSo, the number of pages she should read tomorrow is:\n96 / 2 = 48 pages\nTherefore, Julie should read 48 pages tomorrow.<|end_turn|>"
